WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.888 --> 00:00:02.720
Film Courage: How do you write good dialogue?

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.760
Shane Stanley, Filmmaker/Author/Instructor:&nbsp;
I think the best dialogue comes from the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:13.600
listeners. I had an absolute crash course in&nbsp;
writing dialogue. Years ago I used to write with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:20.480
Charlie Sheen. I don’t care if they don’t credit&nbsp;
him and the sitcom’s he’s been in as a writer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.080
I know who has been writing the dialogue.&nbsp;
I worked with him for three years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.840
I never read or heard better dialogue from anybody&nbsp;
other than that which came from him that he wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:35.600
and I asked him one day. I used to be really good&nbsp;
at writing scene direction and my dialogue was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.800
flat, it wasn’t interesting, it wasn’t&nbsp;
engaging, it wasn’t edge of your seat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.360
I asked him one day when we were writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.720
the way we worked is we’d set it up, I would write&nbsp;
the scene direction, he would do the dialogue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:53.040
unless you know it was basic stuff (it was easy).&nbsp;
I finally said to him I said where do you get this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.920
encyclopedia of incredible dialogue, I’ve never&nbsp;
heard anybody with it before and he said “You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:04.880
just listen.” He said “You know when this is&nbsp;
working [pointing to his mouth], these aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.040
working [pointing to his ears]. And if these&nbsp;
aren’t working [referring to his ears] you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:11.200
can’t absorb what is happening and how&nbsp;
people interact and find those gems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.520
Most of the things that I write that people&nbsp;
like, I heard at a party, I heard at a barbecue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.200
I heard at an airport, I heard it while I was&nbsp;
pumping gas.” He’ll say “I’ll get out and pump gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.240
and I’ll hear a couple bickering at the car next&nbsp;
to me at the pump.” And he says “That’s gold. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.320
is gold man.” And this was before everybody&nbsp;
had phones and we weren’t listening to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:39.680
we were doing this [scrolling].” So I find the&nbsp;
best advice to writing dialogue is listen. Put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:46.400
yourself in a character’s position that you’ve&nbsp;
created. It goes back to the old I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:53.200
keeping the audience interested in good dialogue&nbsp;
I think also is a lot of people try to outwit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:58.320
themselves with dialogue. They think they’ve got&nbsp;
to use verbiage or words that people don’t use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.160
They’ve got to show you how smart they are. I&nbsp;
think we’ve talk about this before, the drummer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:09.680
that overplays everything to show how good my&nbsp;
chops are and it’s not about that, it’s about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.960
making things sound organic and that's a lot of&nbsp;
things that the actors have to understand too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.600
is that they’re acting or they are truly&nbsp;
listening and responding with the work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.000
But I think the best dialogue comes&nbsp;
from the best listeners and the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.440
who know how to implement those words that&nbsp;
they've discovered over the years and knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.800
arenas. Obviously you’re not going be pumping&nbsp;
gas at a gas station and hear a husband and wife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.480
Every script you write is not going to be about a&nbsp;
husband and wife pumping gas at the gas station.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.520
Where else can you implement that? That could be&nbsp;
a film like SAHARA where Matthew McConaughey and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.080
Penélope Cruz are walking across the desert or&nbsp;
you can put some of that bickering in different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.200
situations but it’s how you use it and how&nbsp;
it inspires you to go down different paths.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:03:01.360
That’s how I learned from him to write better&nbsp;
dialogue. I’m still not very good but I know good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.640
dialogue when I hear it and I’m good at adjusting&nbsp;
dialogue when I get it and it’s about listening.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:13.360
Film Courage: And now people are on phones so you&nbsp;
can just hear one side. And I don’t think people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:19.360
realize or if they care how well you can hear them&nbsp;
or how dialed in (no pun intended) the people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.160
are…I mean I’ve heard some major arguments…major&nbsp;
personal things [in public] and I’m like I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:28.640
think this person realizes I can hear them&nbsp;
half way across the store? But that’s gold too.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.240
Shane: It’s gold and it’s even better when&nbsp;
they’re on speaker phones so you do get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:35.440
hear all of it or they’re receiver is&nbsp;
up so loud but you’re right, sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:39.200
you will hear one half of the conversation&nbsp;
and sometimes that could be better because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:43.360
you’re able to imagine…I mean can you imagine&nbsp;
if it was Hey Harold you didn’t take the trash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:46.640
barrels in and that’s what the whole fight is&nbsp;
about but you’re only hearing Harold’s side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.520
can you imagine what you can create?&nbsp;
Maude is yelling to him about?

00:03:51.520 --> 00:04:00.480
Film Courage: When you were working at the car&nbsp;
lot, did you pick up anything about dialogue? I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.616
realize that’s not why you were there [in another&nbsp;
video Shane talks about selling cars for a short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.616 --> 00:04:06.960
time and what it taught him]. But if I’m coming&nbsp;
to look at a car…you know…I’m probably going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.640
to be hesitant. I’m probably going to be like I&nbsp;
don’t want this guy to sell me anything but I do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:15.840
need a car but I don’t want him to know that so&nbsp;
I’m going to be very selective with what I say.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:23.280
Shane: I learned when I said when the owner of the&nbsp;
car lot had said “Come sell cars. You will learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.000
more about life than you will ever known and it&nbsp;
will be the education that you lacked,” he wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.040
kidding. You become a good listener because&nbsp;
you have to overcome all objectives you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:38.000
address every concern and need the customer has.&nbsp;
So how does that happen? It happens by listening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:45.680
and I always…it wasn’t dialogue lines and I have&nbsp;
a very retentive memory if I hear something I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:54.880
remember it. There wasn’t anything said in those&nbsp;
18 months I worked there that stood out but it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:01.440
mannerisms, it was quirks, it was&nbsp;
character flaws or neat things about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:08.160
people that I still do this day will implement&nbsp;
in my characters. The way a husband and wife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:14.000
interact. It’s not about what’s said, it’s about&nbsp;
how it’s said, it’s about the posture. It’s about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:22.320
the 45-year-old son who is with his 70-year-old&nbsp;
parents buying the Ford Taurus today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.800
and you realize that the parents have a gambling&nbsp;
addiction and they’re getting a car to drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.280
to Vegas every other week but the 45-year-old&nbsp;
son still lives at home to help supplement the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:35.680
overhead so they can afford to maintain&nbsp;
their lifestyle and the kid is kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.320
a deadbeat too. I mean there are all sorts of&nbsp;
and you look at that and you go that's a story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:47.200
You’ve got this retired milkman and his wife&nbsp;
who ran a salon and now they're addicted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.400
going to Vegas every…well what else are they doing&nbsp;
when they go to Vegas that we don’t know about?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:56.880
And then your mind goes what do they see on that&nbsp;
trip to Vegas? You know it’s the Lotto winners. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:03.120
sold to a few Lotto winners. That was interesting.&nbsp;
I actually had a salesman I worked with who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:10.800
won 24 million dollars in the big Lotto and he&nbsp;
left his wife and two and a half years later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.680
he was at the point selling cars with me and you&nbsp;
want to talk about like having it all and losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:22.000
it? Friends who abandoned him when the money&nbsp;
was gone and he said yeah you know it's funny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:27.360
my wife still has her 10 million…you know…and&nbsp;
here I am selling cars trying to make a living,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.120
living in a one-bedroom apartment. And he was the&nbsp;
one who left her and was horrible to her…yeah…you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.240
see a whole bunch of really interesting people…

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:40.480
Film Courage: But that two years&nbsp;
(sorry to interrupt) but that two years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:42.560
was probably very fascinating?

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:48.320
Shane: Fascinating. As I said I’m still very close&nbsp;
to the family that gifted me that opportunity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:55.120
I am still very close to some of the salesman&nbsp;
that I worked with at the lot. Sadly some of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:59.200
have perished. Some of those people in that&nbsp;
sales class that I took Patrick just passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:05.120
six months ago I found out. Stuart who sold&nbsp;
with me and I got the most character stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:10.640
out of anybody in the world, passed. These were&nbsp;
people I worked with and I was very close to and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:19.440
it was amazing just the people, the experiences,&nbsp;
the encounters of just walking out having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.240
cigarette and encountering a guy whose car is in&nbsp;
for an oil change and that 20-minute exchange you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:28.320
have with this guy, those conversations will live&nbsp;
with you forever but you don’t have to sell cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:34.240
to do it. You can drive an Uber, you can work at&nbsp;
a restaurant as a waiter, you can work anywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:37.760
Most of the encounters that I’ve&nbsp;
had in my life that I write about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:42.160
are about are personal experiences or&nbsp;
exchanges or encounters that I’ve had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:47.520
anywhere but it’s funny you bring up the car&nbsp;
years because that was my job five, six days a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:52.880
week. I spend those eight to 12 a day dealing with&nbsp;
the dealership with no two days every the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:59.840
fighting between salesmen for a customer or a&nbsp;
customer…you know there’s an old saying in buying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:05.520
cars - all buyers are liars. And it was about&nbsp;
trying to…I learned something. We used to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:10.640
a salesman that sold unbelievable amount of cars&nbsp;
and made unbelievable amounts of money and I used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:14.000
to say to him “What’s your secret?” And he said&nbsp;
“You’ve just got to remember all buyers are liars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:18.960
and they’re telling you one thing because&nbsp;
they think they can have an edge and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:24.640
secret to successfully selling cars is giving&nbsp;
buyers - they’ve got to trust you, they've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:28.640
to like you and want to deal with you.” But&nbsp;
he said “You have to let them think they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:32.000
control but they really don’t have an ounce of&nbsp;
it.” And it's almost like being a film director.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:39.600
Actors live to be directed and controlled&nbsp;
but they also like to think that they’re the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.000
show. And a good director will help steer them&nbsp;
and let them think that they can run with it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.320
and you've got to be able to reign them in without&nbsp;
them knowing they’re being reigned in. It’s an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:54.560
art, it’s a dance and it’s that way selling cars.&nbsp;
But it taught me how to negotiate, selling cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:59.520
and negotiating four, five, six times a day&nbsp;
sure taught me how to go out and raise money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:04.160
sure taught me how to negotiate with contracts&nbsp;
and actors and agents. It’s the same thing. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:21.840
much for how much? So everybody should go sell&nbsp;
cars and you will become a better filmmaker.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:29.120
Question for the viewers: Where does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:30.147
your best dialogue come from?

